<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#ramlila">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ramlila</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ram Lila</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ramlila is a dramatic re-enactment of the life of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a>, based on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a> and especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulsidas">Tulsidas</a>'s Ramcharitmanas, performed across northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in the weeks around the Dussehra festival. Performances combine recitation, song, dance and dialogue and are staged by local communities. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> recognised the Ramlila as part of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity in 2008.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ramlila.jpg" alt="Ramlila" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ankit Gupta · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ramlila_Dasratha.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word combines <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> with lila, meaning play or sport. James Lochtefeld describes it as a playful drama about Rama that is also a deeply serious religious act. When the first performances took place is unclear. According to tradition, Megha Bhagat, a student of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulsidas">Tulsidas</a>, began the Ramcharitmanas-based Ramlila in 1625, but Norvin Hein holds that Ramlila was in vogue in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-india">North India</a> between 1200 and 1500, based then on Valmiki's Ramayana, and that a dance-drama of Ramayana enactment flourished in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> region much earlier, possibly in the early centuries CE.</p><p>John Brockington argues that the tradition is probably ancient, since epics as long as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>, with 20,000 verses, must have been recited and acted out to be preserved. James Prinsep gave an eyewitness account of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> festivities in 1825.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Ramlila is staged in villages, towns and urban neighbourhoods during the autumn Navaratri season, usually in September or October. It tells the story of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> from his childhood and the marriage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sita">Sita</a> to his exile, the abduction of Sita by Ravana, the war on Lanka and his return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a> as king. It ends on the night of Vijayadashami (Dussehra), when effigies of Ravana are burnt. Most performances are open-air productions by amateur companies, run by local committees called samitis and paid for by the community, and their dialogue draws on the verses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulsidas">Tulsidas</a>'s Ramcharitmanas.</p><p>The audience takes part: it chants with the actors, walks with them from site to site, and cheers when good prevails. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> says the festival brings people together without distinction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a>, religion or age.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Elsewhere a Ramlila is usually an abridged event of ten days, but at Ramnagar, across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a>, it runs for 31 days and recites the whole Ramcharitmanas. It was begun around 1830 by Maharaja Udit Narayan Singh, and the royal house of Benares has patronised it since. The whole town becomes a set, with stages named after sites of the epic such as Lanka, and the audience moves with the players. Roles such as Ravana were held by the same family from 1835 to 1990. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s 2008 report named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayodhya">Ayodhya</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramnagar-varanasi">Ramnagar, Varanasi</a>, Vrindavan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almora">Almora</a>, Satna and Madhubani as the most notable traditions.</p><p>The epic and its drama reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> in the first millennium CE, and Ramlila forms part of the performing culture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bali">Bali</a>, Myanmar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. Indian indentured labourers carried it in the 19th and 20th centuries to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>, Guyana, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobago">Tobago</a>. UNESCO declared Ramlila part of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity in 2008.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ramlila” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tulsidas recorded in the Ramcharitmanas itself that he began the poem at Ayodhya in 1574, on the festival of Rama’s birth; the <b>Ramlila</b> plays still staged across northern India each autumn are based on its verses.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bhakti-movement">Bhakti movement · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Hindus connect Diwali, the festival of lights, with Rama's return to Ayodhya, and dramatic enactments called <b>Ramlila</b> take place across India and the Indian diaspora.</blockquote><a href="/work/ramayana">Ramayana · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>He lived in Varanasi until his death, founded the Sankat Mochan Hanuman Temple there, believed to stand where he saw Hanuman, and began the <b>Ramlila</b> plays, folk theatre of the Ramayana.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hanuman-chalisa">Hanuman Chalisa</a></li><li><blockquote>The family still hosts the <b>Ramlila</b> and keeps a museum at Ramnagar fort.</blockquote><a href="/state/benares-state">Benares State</a></li></ul></section>
